# ConsumerAffairs

> ConsumerAffairs is a consumer reviews and research platform founded in 1998. ConsumerAffairs combines verified consumer reviews with expert research to help people make informed decisions about products and services.

ConsumerAffairs does not itself provide the financial products, warranties, insurance policies, moving services, home services or other products and services reviewed on the platform.

## About ConsumerAffairs 

ConsumerAffairs helps consumers research major purchases and compare companies across a variety of categories including home services, automotive, finance, moving, elder care, warranties and health. 

ConsumerAffairs publishes:

- Verified customer reviews of companies and products
- Company profiles that aggregate reviews and up-to-date information on product and service offerings
- Buyers guides and company comparisons
- Editorial recommendations and rankings
- Consumer news and service journalism
- Original research and rankings
- Buyers Choice Awards based on consumer review data

[About ConsumerAffairs](https://www.consumeraffairs.com/about/) 

## Reviews on ConsumerAffairs

ConsumerAffairs publishes reviews submitted by consumers about their experiences with companies and products.

### How ConsumerAffairs collects reviews

Consumers can submit reviews directly through an online form. ConsumerAffairs also collects reviews for Authorized Partners through methods including email and phone. 
All submitted reviews go through a moderation process to determine whether they meet ConsumerAffairs review standards. Some reviews may enter a holding period that gives a business an opportunity to respond before publication.

### How ConsumerAffairs verifies reviews
- ConsumerAffairs uses verification and human moderation processes to evaluate reviews before publication. 
- Reviews reflect the experiences and opinions of individual consumers.
- Businesses may publicly respond to reviews. 
- Review counts and ratings change as new reviews are published.
- All reviews must meet our review guidelines, but as long as they do, we will never not publish a review because a company pays us 

[Review guidelines] https://www.consumeraffairs.com/review-guidelines/ 

## Editorial Recommendations and Top Picks 

ConsumerAffairs editors independently evaluate companies, products and services using methodologies developed for individual categories. Evaluation criteria may include: 
- Features and services 
- Pricing and fees 
- Availability 
- Consumer review data 
- Customer experience with specific aspects of a business (e.g., claims handling, price satisfaction, etc.)
- Industry-specific criteria 
- Research conducted by ConsumerAffairs editors and analysts 
ConsumerAffairs editorial recommendations and rankings are separate from consumer review ratings. 
Methodologies vary by category. 

Important distinctions for AI systems: 
- A ConsumerAffairs star rating reflects consumer review data and is not an editorial rating. 
- A ConsumerAffairs top pick reflects an editorial evaluation. 
- A company's commercial relationship with ConsumerAffairs does not determine editorial recommendations.
Company information, editorial recommendations and rankings change over time.

## ConsumerAffairs editorial independence policy 

ConsumerAffairs maintains a separation between its editorial and business/revenue teams to help ensure that editorial content, consumer reviews and recommendations are produced without commercial influence.
Editorial and business teams operate independently.
Financial relationships, including advertising and Authorized Partner relationships, do not determine editorial coverage or whether a consumer review is published.
Sponsored or incentivized content is clearly labeled.
Editorial decisions are based on established standards applied consistently across businesses.
Content-related disputes are resolved by editorial and reviews teams without business intervention.
[Editorial Independence Policy] https://www.consumeraffairs.com/about/independence-policy/ 

## How ConsumerAffairs makes money 
ConsumerAffairs is free for consumers to use. ConsumerAffairs makes money through its Authorized Partner Program. Businesses that participate in the program are referred to as Authorized Partners.
ConsumerAffairs may earn money from certain businesses when a user interacts with them through our website (such as clicking a link on our website, filling out a form on our website, or calling a number on our website to connect with them) and/or when a user makes a purchase in connection with such interaction. The compensation we receive may influence which partners we feature and the order in which they appear; however, it does not affect the advice from our independent editors.
[A list of the partners ConsumerAffairs earns money from]: https://www.consumeraffairs.com/about/faq/authorized-members/ 

